### Cline Surface

CLI

### Cline Version

CLI 3.0.73 (`main` at `1619168`)

### Beta version

- [ ] I am using a beta version of Cline

### What happened?

The interactive MCP wizard (`apps/cli/src/wizards/mcp/index.ts`) splits the stdio command with `parseStdioCommand` and stores `parts[0]` as `command` and the rest as `args`.

`parseStdioCommand` treats every backslash as an escape character, inside double quotes and outside quotes alike, and drops it:

```ts
if (char === "\\") {
  escaping = true;
  continue;
}
```

On Windows, every path the user types loses its separators. A server added with `node C:\Users\me\mcp\server.js` is saved with the argument `C:Usersmemcpserver.js`, so the server fails to start because the file doesn't exist. Quoting the path, as most users do for `Program Files`, does not help, because escapes are processed inside double quotes too.

### Possible Solution

1. Treat a backslash as an escape only when it comes before a quote character, or before whitespace outside quotes (`my\ dir`).
2. Keep any other backslash literally, and never process escapes inside single quotes.
3. Add Windows-path cases next to the existing `parses quoted stdio command arguments` test in `settings.test.ts`.

```ts
export function parseStdioCommand(input: string): string[] {
  const tokens: string[] = [];
  let current = "";
  let quote: '"' | "'" | undefined;
  const chars = [...input.trim()];
  for (let i = 0; i < chars.length; i++) {
    const char = chars[i]!;
    // A backslash escapes only a quote or whitespace, so Windows paths such as
    // C:\Users\me\server.js keep their separators.
    if (char === "\\" && quote !== "'") {
      const next = chars[i + 1];
      if (next !== undefined && (next === '"' || next === "'" || (!quote && /\s/.test(next)))) {
        current += next;
        i++;
        continue;
      }
      current += char;
      continue;
    }
    if (quote) {
      if (char === quote) quote = undefined;
      else current += char;
      continue;
    }
    if (char === '"' || char === "'") { quote = char; continue; }
    if (/\s/.test(char)) {
      if (current) { tokens.push(current); current = ""; }
      continue;
    }
    current += char;
  }
  if (current) tokens.push(current);
  return tokens;
}
```

With this version:

- Both commands above keep their backslashes.
- `node my\ dir/server.js --name "a \"b\""` still gives `["node","my dir/server.js","--name","a \"b\""]`.
- The existing `npx -y "@scope/server name" --root "my dir"` case still gives the same result.

I'm happy to send a PR.

### Steps to reproduce

Call the parser the wizard uses:

```ts
parseStdioCommand(String.raw`node C:\Users\me\mcp\server.js`)
// ["node","C:Usersmemcpserver.js"]

parseStdioCommand(String.raw`"C:\Program Files\nodejs\node.exe" C:\tools\server.js`)
// ["C:Program Filesnodejsnode.exe","C:toolsserver.js"]
```

The same happens through `cline mcp` â†’ add server â†’ stdio, entering either command above.

Expected:

```
["node","C:\\Users\\me\\mcp\\server.js"]
["C:\\Program Files\\nodejs\\node.exe","C:\\tools\\server.js"]
```

### Provider/Model

Not applicable.

### Diagnostics

Reproduced by running `parseStdioCommand` from source with `npx tsx`.

### System Information

Linux 6.12 (Debian 13), Node.js 20.19.2. The parser is platform-independent, but the problem affects Windows paths.
